#
# prima-materia — whether the action reaches a subject who does not know of it
#
# One protocol. It is written because the claim it examines is, uniquely in
# this holding, one that a commission with a budget actually tested, with a
# design good enough to be worth reporting two centuries later — and because
# the transmission being examined forbids exactly that design.
#
# The evidence was read at the wording in the English translation named below,
# not remembered. The counter-search was carried and came back with opposition,
# which is recorded in the note rather than smoothed over.
#

pmp:MagneticActionWithoutKnowledgeExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the magnetic action reaches a subject who does not know it is being applied"@en, "Ob die magnetische Wirkung einen Menschen erreicht, der nichts von ihr weiss"@de ;
    skos:definition "The examined claim is the one the transmission needs in order not to be an account of expectation: that the operator's action produces its effects in the subject, and produces them at the places to which it is directed, independently of the subject's belief that it is being applied. The manual read for traditions/mesmerism.ttl asserts it in the form that confidence is necessary in the operator and not in the person operated upon, and that one can act equally upon those who believe and those who do not."@en ;
    skos:note "The design the commissioners used is the one that answers it and it is worth stating as a design, because it is better than the century it comes from. A bandage was made of two rubber cups filled with eiderdown, sewn into circles of cloth and tied behind the head, leaving room for the nose and full liberty of breathing and admitting no light above, below or through. Subjects already known to be sensible to the operation were blindfolded; for the most part they were not operated upon at all, and were put questions framed so as to point out the answer. The commissioners state the logic themselves: where no operation was performed the sole answer ought to have been that no sensation was felt, and where it was performed the impression and not the form of the question ought to have dictated the reply."@en ;
    skos:note "Four results, all read at the wording. A woman who had been the one of eleven persons sensible to one operator's action reported, blindfolded, sensations that no longer answered to the places towards which the action was directed: operated upon the stomach and the back she felt only heat in her head and pain in both eyes and the left ear. Blindfolded again with the operator removed, silence enjoined and the subject induced to believe the operation was being performed, the effects were the same though no operation near or distant was performed; and when after a quarter of an hour a sign was made for the operation to begin she felt nothing, the sensations diminishing instead of augmenting. A servant persuaded that he was being operated upon felt a nearly universal heat, emotions in the belly, extreme heaviness, and grew drowsy. A medical man believing the operation had been proceeding for ten minutes before it had begun felt a heat in his loins he compared to a stove, and felt nothing on several occasions when it was performed."@en ;
    skos:note "The fifth is the one usually remembered and it belongs here because of who chose the subject. The commissioners required that the trial be made on a person of the practitioner's own choosing whose susceptibility he was already convinced of; he brought a boy of about twelve. An apricot tree in Franklin's orchard at Passy, standing clear of the others, was operated upon by the practitioner alone. The boy, blindfolded, was brought to four trees that had not been operated upon and made to embrace each for two minutes, the mode of communication the practitioner had himself prescribed, with commissioners standing between him and the boy so that no signal could pass. At the first tree the boy reported perspiring in large drops, coughing, spitting and a slight pain in the head; at the second stupefaction and head pain; at the third the two increased and he said he believed he was approaching the operated tree; at the fourth, some twenty-four feet from it, he fell into a crisis and fainted."@en ;
    skos:note "The conclusion is stated in the form that makes it evidence and not opinion, and it is quoted here because the wording is the finding: that the imagination without the magnetism produces convulsions, and that the magnetism without the imagination produces nothing. The commissioners add that the existence of the fluid is destitute of proof, that having no existence it can have no use, and — an observation of their own, and a claim in its own right that this protocol does not examine — that the compressions and the repeated action of the imagination may be hurtful and that every public process employing these means must in the end produce pernicious effects."@en ;
    skos:note "Why the state is not stronger than it is, and the reason is a date and a person. This commission examined one practitioner's practice in 1784; the manual examined in traditions/mesmerism.ttl was written in 1825 by a man who had read the report, refers to 1784, and holds that at that time the true principles were not yet known. The two are not the same operator and not the same method, and the commission's subjects were the practitioner's own choice under the older doctrine. The blindfold design nevertheless tests the claim and not the doctrine, because the claim is that the action reaches a subject who does not know of it, and that is what the bandage removes. Deviation was found, in five subjects, with a design whose outcome could have gone the other way, and that is what this state asserts."@en ;
    skos:note "The three checks SPEC requires of an evidence citation, carried one at a time. The work is named with translator, place, printer and year from its own title page, read as a whole. It supports the claim it is cited for and not a neighbouring one: the blindfold series is about the reach of the action and not about whether the cures happened, and this protocol says nothing about the cures. The population is the commission's own — eleven persons under one operator, of whom one was sensible; the servant and the medical man at that operator's house; and the boy of twelve chosen by the practitioner who was being examined. That last is the strongest single feature of the design and the one a reader should weigh: the subject was selected by the party with an interest in success."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:examinedBy "Commission of the Faculty of Medicine and the Royal Academy of Sciences, Paris 1784 — Franklin, Bailly, Lavoisier, Majault, Sallin, d'Arcet, de Bory, Le Roy and Borie; protocol entered by prima-materia, 2026-09-14" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-14" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A blindfold series of the same form in which the reported sensations follow the places to which the action is directed, and in which subjects falsely told the operation is proceeding report nothing. The claim under examination is that the action reaches a subject who does not know of it, so a series in which the report tracks the action rather than the belief falls due in its favour and against this protocol."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "Report of Dr. Benjamin Franklin, and Other Commissioners, Charged by the King of France, with the Examination of the Animal Magnetism, as now Practised at Paris, translated from the French, with an historical introduction (London: J. Johnson, 1785), the blindfold experiments and the conclusions" ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched for, and found at the wording: the transmission's own reply, in the work read for traditions/mesmerism.ttl, that at the time Mesmer first connected patients around the baquet he knew neither how to direct the agent nor how to calm the crises, that since 1784 the true principles being known the scenes which then made so much noise are no longer reproduced, and that the convulsive effects arise where the practice is used out of curiosity, to exhibit power or to obtain singular effects. That is a genuine objection to the generality of the 1784 conclusion and it is the reason this protocol's state is not stronger. Searched for and not opened, therefore named here and not counted: Jussieu's separate publication as the member of the parallel commission of the Société royale de médecine who refused to sign; the reports of the Academy of Medicine of 1826 and 1831, which reached the opposite conclusion and of whose printed text the collating witness of 1841 alleges notable variations from Husson's manuscript; and Bertrand's account of 1826. Each is a party with standing and each is reachable; until one of them is read at the wording this protocol reports one commission and says so."@en ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "The commissioners are themselves a party and the file says where. They examined the practice of one practitioner and drew a conclusion about the magnetism in general, and they answer the objection in a footnote by appeal to the king's intention rather than to their design. Their historical section, which rests on a separate enquiry by another hand, concludes that the doctrine is an old falsehood revived, and their closing paragraph is a recommendation about public practice. None of that is evidence and none of it is counted here; what is counted is the blindfold series."@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 26, p. 26, and chap. VIII, pp. 170-171" .
